---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const collCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'm1', label: 'mass 1', min: 0.5, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: 'kg' },
    { name: 'm2', label: 'mass 2', min: 0.5, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 6, unit: 'kg' },
    { name: 'u1', label: 'velocity of 1', min: -10, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 6, unit: 'm/s' },
    { name: 'u2', label: 'velocity of 2', min: -10, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 0, unit: 'm/s' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'momentum before (m1u1 + m2u2)', expr: 'm1*u1 + m2*u2', unit: 'kg·m/s' },
    { label: 'velocity of 1 after (elastic)', expr: '((m1 - m2)*u1 + 2*m2*u2)/(m1 + m2)', unit: 'm/s' },
    { label: 'velocity of 2 after (elastic)', expr: '((m2 - m1)*u2 + 2*m1*u1)/(m1 + m2)', unit: 'm/s' },
    { label: 'both stuck together (inelastic)', expr: '(m1*u1 + m2*u2)/(m1 + m2)', unit: 'm/s' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/momentum-and-collisions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Momentum</strong> — mass times velocity, p = mv — is the quantity nature refuses to
    let disappear. In every collision, explosion or rocket burn, the total momentum before
    equals the total after. The reason is hiding in Newton's second law, which in its original
    form says force is the <em>rate of change of momentum</em>: F = Δp/Δt. When two objects
    collide, the forces between them are equal and opposite (Newton's third law) and act for
    exactly the same time, so their momentum changes are equal and opposite — whatever one
    loses, the other gains. With no <em>outside</em> force, the system's total cannot drift.
    Energy may change form; momentum just moves around. That asymmetry — one exact bookkeeping
    rule that survives even when energy does not — is what makes momentum the workhorse of
    crash analysis, rocketry and particle physics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a 2 kg cart at 6 m/s meets a 6 kg cart at rest — compare the elastic and stuck-together outcomes" widget="sliderLab" config={collCfg} />

  <p>
    Two outcomes bracket reality. <strong>Elastic</strong> (billiard balls): both momentum and
    kinetic energy survive — watch the heavy cart take some speed and the light one bounce
    back. <strong>Inelastic</strong> (clay balls): momentum is still conserved exactly, but
    kinetic energy is lost to crumpling — both carts roll on together at the readout's last
    value. Make the striking mass tiny (m1 = 0.5) and see how little it budges a heavy target;
    make it huge and the target has no choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Conservation in action: the worked collision</h2>
  <p>
    Run the widget's default case by hand. A 2 kg cart at 6 m/s strikes a 6 kg cart at rest.
    Momentum before: 2 × 6 + 6 × 0 = <strong>12 kg·m/s</strong> — and 12 it stays.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, a note on method, because it explains why physicists reach for this law first.
    During a real crash the contact force is unknowable — it spikes wildly for milliseconds as
    materials deform, and no force meter survives it. Momentum bookkeeping skips the messy
    middle entirely: <em>before and after suffice</em>. The second skill is sign discipline.
    Momentum is a vector, so in one-dimensional problems pick a positive direction and stick to
    it: a 4 kg cart moving left at 5 m/s carries p = −20 kg·m/s. Two carts approaching each
    other, 4 kg at +5 and 6 kg at −3 m/s, carry +20 − 18 = +2 kg·m/s total; if they couple,
    the wreckage moves right at 2/10 = 0.2 m/s — the 4 kg cart's surplus, made visible by
    bookkeeping. Head-on collisions that "stop dead" are the special case where the momenta
    exactly cancel, not a violation of conservation but its tidiest exhibit.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Perfectly inelastic</strong> (they couple): the combined 8 kg must carry all
    12, so v = 12/8 = <strong>1.5 m/s</strong>. But check kinetic energy: before,
    ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 J; after, ½ × 8 × 1.5² = 9 J. Twenty-seven joules — three quarters — went
    into crumpling, heating and sound. Momentum kept perfect books; energy changed currency.</li>
    <li><strong>Elastic</strong> (both momentum and KE conserved) needs two equations, and
    gives v₁ = ((m₁ − m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂) = (−4 × 6)/8 = <strong>−3 m/s</strong> — the
    light cart bounces backwards — and v₂ = 2m₁u₁/(m₁ + m₂) = 24/8 = <strong>3 m/s</strong>.
    Energy check: ½·2·3² + ½·6·3² = 9 + 27 = 36 J. ✓ Books balance in both currencies.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two limits are worth naming. The "heavy strikes light" elastic case (m₁ above m₂) makes
    the light object fly off at nearly <em>double</em> the incoming speed — this is why a golf
    club head (heavy) can launch a ball (light) faster than the club itself moves, and why a
    Newton's cradle's end ball exits at the incoming speed. The "light strikes heavy" case
    barely moves the target, which is why you can bat away a ping-pong ball with a bowling
    ball, but not vice versa.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "momentum is conserved, so kinetic energy must be too —
  they're both 'motion stuff'." No: momentum conservation is exact in <em>every</em> collision
  of an isolated system; kinetic energy conservation is a special property of elastic ones.
  The stuck-together case above is the cleanest counterexample: 12 kg·m/s before and after,
  but 36 J became 9 J. The lost KE isn't destroyed — it's thermal and acoustic energy, which
  the thermodynamics chapter tracks. Exams love the pair of them together; solving a collision
  usually means <em>deciding which</em> of the two is conserved.</p>

  <h2>Impulse: the force–time trade</h2>
  <p>
    Rearrange Newton's second law: <strong>F·Δt = Δp</strong> — impulse equals momentum
    change. The same momentum change can be bought with a large force briefly or a small force
    over a long time, and every safety technology in your life is this equation wearing a
    product. Worked example: a 1,200 kg car braking to rest from 15 m/s must shed
    Δp = 18,000 kg·m/s. Into a rigid wall in 0.1 s: average force 180,000 N — about 15 g.
    Into a crumpling barrier over 0.5 s: 36,000 N — 3 g, survivable. Same Δp, fourfold longer
    time, quarter the force. Airbags, crumple zones, crash mats, helmets, running-shoe soles
    and the bend in your knees when you land from a jump are all impulse engineering: extend
    the stopping time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sport runs the same equation forwards and backwards. A tennis player <em>follows
    through</em> to keep applying force over a longer contact time, buying more Δp (more
    speed) for the same peak force. A boxer <em>rides</em> a punch, moving the head back with
    the blow to stretch Δt and shrink F. And the distinction between catching a ball and
    trapping it against your chest is purely the contact time. In every case the momentum
    change is fixed by the situation; the pain is negotiable. The same arithmetic prices
    everyday risk: a fall from 3 m delivers the same Δp whether you land on a crash mat
    (Δt ≈ 0.2 s) or concrete (Δt ≈ 0.01 s) — twenty times the contact time is twenty times
    less force, which is the whole content of "cushioned".
  </p>

  <h2>Explosions and rockets: conservation with nothing to push on</h2>
  <p>
    An explosion is a collision run in reverse: initially one object at rest, finally many
    objects whose momenta sum to zero. A 60 kg skater standing still throws a 3 kg ball
    forward at 10 m/s: the ball carries 30 kg·m/s forward, so the skater must carry 30 kg·m/s
    backward — a recoil of 30/60 = <strong>0.5 m/s</strong>. No friction, no mystery: the
    centre of mass never moved.
  </p>
  <p>
    A rocket is the skater, continuously. It throws propellant backward at high speed and
    gains forward momentum — <em>it does not push against the air</em>, which is why rockets
    work superbly in vacuum (better, actually: no back-pressure). The practical difficulty is
    that the "skater" must carry all its own balls: the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation shows the
    speed gained grows with the logarithm of the mass ratio, which is why rockets are mostly
    fuel tanks and why multi-stage designs (drop the empty tank, shrink m) exist. Every
    spacecraft manoeuvre, from Apollo to station reboosts, is momentum conservation with
    nothing to brace against.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading real crashes</h2>
  <p>
    Momentum thinking also reorganises your intuitions about danger. A 30,000 kg truck at
    1 m/s carries 30,000 kg·m/s; a 100 kg cyclist at 30 m/s carries only 3,000 — the truck's
    stop is <em>harder to buy</em> despite its gentler speed, which is why stopping distance
    scales with speed <em>squared</em> when you're spending the kinetic energy, but linearly
    when you're only neutralising the momentum. Meanwhile the movie trope of a hero knocked
    backward ten metres by a pistol shot fails conservation instantly: a 10 g bullet at
    400 m/s carries 4 kg·m/s; sharing that with an 80 kg person gives them 0.05 m/s — a
    nudge, not a flight. Momentum arithmetic is lie detection for action films.
  </p>
  <p>
    And one caution about cause and effect: the forces in a crash are third-law pairs — the
    truck hits the car exactly as hard as the car hits the truck — but the <em>outcomes</em>
    differ because the masses differ. Equal force on 30,000 kg is a slight deceleration; on
    1,000 kg, catastrophe. "Which hit harder" is the wrong question; "which changed velocity
    more" is the physics, and it is answered by the momentum ledger, not by the damage.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The centre of mass never flinches.</strong> Define the centre of mass of the
    system — the mass-weighted average position — and the theorem reads: with no external
    force, the centre of mass moves at constant velocity, through collisions, explosions and
    acrobatics alike. A diver spinning in mid-air can contort every limb but cannot move her
    centre of mass from the parabola the projectile chapter drew; a firework's fragments,
    considered as a system, keep flying as one invisible body (drag aside). This is the
    deepest form of conservation: not "things carry on" but "the average carries on".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The centre-of-mass frame makes collisions almost easy.</strong> Switch to a frame
    moving with the centre of mass, and in that frame the total momentum is zero — before
    <em>and</em> after. An elastic collision in this frame simply <em>reverses</em> each
    object's velocity; switching back recovers the formulas in the widget's readouts with far
    less algebra. For equal masses colliding elastically in two dimensions (billiard balls),
    the frame argument yields an elegant bonus: after the collision the two balls' velocities
    are perpendicular — watch a pool table long enough to see the 90° law for yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Coefficient of restitution: grading the bounciness.</strong> Real collisions sit
    between the widget's two extremes. The coefficient of restitution e — relative speed of
    separation divided by relative speed of approach — is 1 for perfectly elastic, 0 for
    perfectly inelastic: a basketball ≈ 0.8, a tennis ball ≈ 0.75, a clay lump ≈ 0. The lost
    kinetic energy fraction is 1 − e², so that "80% bounce" is actually returning only 64% of
    the energy as bounce height, the rest spent deforming the ball's rubber (and warming it —
    bounce a ball a hundred times and feel the difference). Standardised bounce tests are
    really standardised e measurements.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Momentum, fields and the quantum world.</strong> The conservation law is broader
    than the collisions: an electromagnetic wave carries momentum (light sails exist, and
    "photon momentum" is how solar sails and laser-tweezers push matter), and in particle
    physics momentum conservation is the bookkeeper that discovers invisible things —
    neutrinos were <em>postulated</em> in 1930 because beta-decay energy and momentum
    accounts didn't balance without a missing particle (found, 1956). In relativity, momentum
    survives as one component of a four-dimensional quantity, and its conservation follows
    from the symmetry of space itself (Noether's theorem: every symmetry buys a conservation
    law — momentum is what you get from "the laws don't care where you are"). The quantity
    Newton could merely define well turns out to be stitched into the geometry of the
    universe.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Newton, Principia (1687); OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 8, Linear Momentum and Collisions); NASA Glenn Research Center rocket equation pages; Open Science note on neutrino history (Cowen, Physics World).</p>
</TopicPage>
